# Rank 1252 / CVE-2026-42557 (GHSA-mqcg-5x36-vfcg): JupyterLab's HTML
# sanitizer allowlisted `data-commandlinker-command` / `data-commandlinker-args`
# on `<button>` elements, and `CommandLinker` registered a single delegated
# click listener on `document.body` that walked up from `event.target`,
# read those two attributes off whatever element was clicked, and executed
# the named command with zero check that the element came from trusted
# JupyterLab UI rather than from sanitized (but attacker-authored) notebook
# output. A saved notebook with an HTML cell output containing a button
# carrying those two data attributes triggered an arbitrary JupyterLab
# command -- including kernel code execution -- on a single click, no code
# cell execution required (CWE-79).
#
# Pool commit 5d9cb8c634e0 carries nocommit; read per Step 0. Its diff (git
# show, packages/apputils/src/commandlinker.ts and .../sanitizer.ts among 23
# files) is the real fix: `_evtClick` becomes async and now calls
# `_isTrusted(target)`, walking the target's ancestors against a WeakMap
# (`_trustedBoundaries`) populated only in-memory by the new `markTrusted()`
# API; when untrusted it shows a confirmation dialog
# (`_requestTrust`/`showCommandLinkerTrustDialog`) before executing, or asks
# the caller to run a `trustCommand` first. `sanitizer.ts` adds a
# `setAllowCommandLinker(false)` toggle (default still `true`) that drops
# the two data attributes from the `button` allowlist entirely. The GHSA
# advisory's own remediation commit, c5f522db11d0, is the same fix rebased
# onto the 4.5.x release branch (identical diff to commandlinker.ts and
# sanitizer.ts, differing only in unrelated version-bump/tooling files from
# being applied at a different point in history) -- confirms 5d9cb8c634e0 is
# the genuine upstream fix, not a version bump, and no pool correction is
# needed.
#
# ATTENTION, precision cost. vyql match on a trimmed but verbatim repro of
# `_evtClick` (event.target walk, hasAttribute/getAttribute on
# `data-commandlinker-command`/`-args`, `this._commands.execute(command,
# args)`) labels no source at all on either revision: `getAttribute()` on a
# DOM element is not bound anywhere in the JavaScript/TypeScript data as a
# taint source, so the `.execute(...)` call -- which does match an existing
# sink binding, coincidentally as code.SqlExecution purely off the method
# name `execute` rather than as any command-dispatch concept -- never fires,
# since a sink with no source reaching it reports nothing. vyql resolve
# confirms `target.hasAttribute` and `event.preventDefault` terminate
# unresolved (not a known source/sink/control); vyql explain reports no
# findings. A bounded scan of the real packages/apputils checkout (--cache
# off --max-ram 4GB, 42-43 javascript files) reports the identical 3
# findings on both the vulnerable and patched revisions (two VYQL-CRY-003 in
# windowresolver.ts, one VYQL-INJ-009 in toolbar/factory.ts), none touching
# commandlinker.ts or sanitizer.ts.
#
# No binding closes this without an unacceptable precision cost. The two
# concepts nearest by theme, code.HtmlSanitizerLogicReview ("review HTML
# sanitizer/parser raw-text, comment-preservation, and serialization
# behavior for sanitizer-bypass or mutation-XSS cases") and
# code.UnsafeEmbeddableAllowlist, both describe a sanitizer emitting
# attacker-reachable markup, not a sanitizer whose *permitted* markup is
# safe on its own but becomes dangerous only because unrelated application
# code (a global click delegate) later trusts one of its allowed attributes
# as a command to execute -- the sanitizer here isn't bypassed or mutated,
# it does exactly what its allowlist says. Emitting a source from any
# `element.getAttribute()`/`.dataset` read inside a document- or
# window-level delegated event handler would be the only way to name the
# actual data flow, but that exact shape -- a global click/keyup handler
# reading a `data-*` attribute off `event.target` to decide what to do --
# is the ordinary, well-documented way Bootstrap's `data-toggle`, htmx's
# `hx-*`, and countless other UI libraries and hand-rolled admin panels
# wire up event delegation; it is not a documented "untrusted input" source
# for any of them, and a security engineer unfamiliar with this CVE would
# not derive "attribute read in a global click handler is tainted" from
# sanitize-html's or DOM's own documentation. The genuinely CVE-specific
# fact -- that this one attribute pair specifically means "execute a
# privileged command" -- is app architecture, not API surface, so naming it
# in a binding would be a repro-shaped patch matching nothing else in the
# corpus, while generalizing it to any DOM-attribute read in a delegated
# handler would flag ordinary, safe event-delegation code broadly. No
# definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost, both revisions reject
# VYQL-INJ-009 (the sink-adjacent DOM injection rule) and reject_review
# code.HtmlSanitizerLogicReview (nearest review concept by theme).

test "rank1252: _evtClick dispatches a data-commandlinker-command attribute with no trust check"
  lang javascript
  file commandlinker.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-009
  reject_review code.HtmlSanitizerLogicReview
  code
  ```
  class CommandLinker {
    private _evtClick(event: MouseEvent): void {
      let target = event.target as HTMLElement;
      while (target && target.parentElement) {
        if (target.hasAttribute('data-commandlinker-command')) {
          event.preventDefault();
          const command = target.getAttribute('data-commandlinker-command');
          if (!command) {
            return;
          }
          const argsValue = target.getAttribute('data-commandlinker-args');
          let args = {};
          if (argsValue) {
            args = JSON.parse(argsValue);
          }
          void this._commands.execute(command, args);
          return;
        }
        target = target.parentElement;
      }
    }

    private _commands: any;
  }
  ```

test "rank1252: _evtClick asks for trust before dispatching an unmarked node"
  lang javascript
  file commandlinker.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-009
  reject_review code.HtmlSanitizerLogicReview
  code
  ```
  class CommandLinker {
    private async _evtClick(event: MouseEvent): Promise<void> {
      let target = event.target as HTMLElement;
      while (target && target.parentElement) {
        if (target.hasAttribute('data-commandlinker-command')) {
          event.preventDefault();
          const command = target.getAttribute('data-commandlinker-command');
          if (!command) {
            return;
          }
          const argsValue = target.getAttribute('data-commandlinker-args');
          let args = {};
          if (argsValue) {
            args = JSON.parse(argsValue);
          }

          if (!this._isTrusted(target)) {
            const trustAction = await this._requestTrust(command, args);
            if (trustAction === 'cancel') {
              return;
            }
          }

          void this._commands.execute(command, args);
          return;
        }
        target = target.parentElement;
      }
    }

    private _isTrusted(target: HTMLElement): boolean {
      let node: HTMLElement | null = target;
      while (node) {
        if (this._trustedBoundaries.has(node)) {
          return true;
        }
        node = node.parentElement;
      }
      return false;
    }

    private async _requestTrust(command: string, args: any): Promise<string> {
      return 'cancel';
    }

    private _commands: any;
    private _trustedBoundaries = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, true>();
  }
  ```
